+# Review of the two Copilot architecture analyses
+
+Reviewed 2026-10-24 against local `b` revision `2cf044c`, application revision `5b8aeed`. The supplied analyses do not identify their source branch or commit; their observations cannot be assumed to describe the latest `b`. This is a review and proposed follow-up, not approval or implementation of another migration. Main/dev remain unchanged.
+
+## Verdict
+
+Accept the maintainability diagnosis for `main.js` and `storage.js`, and the recommendation to preserve domain contracts. Do not execute the proposed migration phases as written: they repeat completed screen migration, introduce overlapping ownership locations, and conflict with the measured progressive-startup decision. File organization alone is not evidence of a performance or security defect, and moving files does not establish a speed improvement.
+
+The approved React refactor is complete within [ADR001](../../architecture/decisions/001-progressive-react-startup.md). Further modularization can improve maintenance without restarting that migration or replacing its architecture.
+
+## Findings and disposition
+
+| Copilot recommendation | Current evidence | Review decision |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Split `main.js` | 1,271 lines; imports, shared mutable context, profile/learning/listening orchestration, view transfer and handlers remain together. | **Agree, with revised boundaries.** Extract cohesive capabilities with explicit dependencies. A large replacement `view-state.js`/`handlers.js` merely relocates coupling. Retain a composition root that wires services; bootstrap necessarily performs orchestration. |
+| Split `storage.js` | 924 lines; database setup, migrations, owned CRUD, record/backup validation and multi-store operations. | **Agree cautiously.** Extract pure validation first; preserve the facade, common transaction helpers and atomic cross-store operations. Size alone does not make the existing module unsafe. |
+| Move UI/navigation into React; migrate Settings → guns → History → Drills → practice → Live Listen → calibration | All 13 screens already have typed page owners and component/runtime tests. `screenNavigation.js` and typed route context already implement guarded history. | **Outdated for current b.** Do not redo these phases or replace the router simply to match a conventional folder example. Remaining startup DOM adapters are intentional. |
+| Remove all native rendering / introduce a client `App.tsx` with providers | ADR001 records a 67,811-byte gzip isolated React hydration probe versus the original public 50,807-byte initial-JS budget. Static typed composition and guarded lazy roots are already implemented. | **Reject as the default plan.** An eager root is incompatible with the current measured budget. Lazy composition remains possible, but does not justify deleting needed startup controls or relaxing performance limits. |
+| Separate pure practice/sequence engines from controllers | `practiceSession.js` already implements pure practice transitions; `drillSession.js` implements sequence transitions. Controllers coordinate capture, queues, saving, safety and presentation. | **Mostly already done.** Further isolate controller presentation/ports where this removes actual duplication. Do not create a second engine or reducer that independently reproduces those transitions. |
+| Introduce result serialization | `toPracticeRecord` and `toSessionRecord` already serialize engine state; recovery and validation are present. | **Already present.** Preserve record compatibility and the distinct engines; consolidate only demonstrably identical policies. |
+| Add a central Context/reducer/store | Practice and sequence factories already own private state, operation tokens, pending/saving guards and event/config queues. High-frequency microphone updates have dedicated ports. | **Not justified by the analysis.** No demonstrated missing global-store requirement. Avoid a second source of truth or broadcasting meter/timer changes through a broad provider. Add a subscription bridge only for a concrete consumer. |
+| Put only generic atomic UI in `packages/ui` | Pure reusable domain UI such as PracticeRun/Review, SequenceRun/Review and DrillSetup is deliberately shared by live pages and guides. Service access is outside these components. | **Too restrictive.** Reusability and side-effect ownership matter, not whether a component is an atom. Keep reusable domain presentation; keep capture/storage/workflows out. |
+| Add `apps/web/src/shared/ui` and `shared/storage` alongside existing packages/core | The proposed trees name UI and storage in multiple locations, without defining which implementation owns each responsibility. | **Revise.** One shared UI home (`packages/ui`), one persistence implementation, existing app-specific feature models. Thin adapters must delegate rather than duplicate. |
+| Strengthen structural boundaries | `verify:architecture` checks source/CSS/test/export presence, screen mappings, direct package imports and obvious service calls. ESLint checks unused declarations and Hooks. | **Partly valid.** Boundaries exist, but these checks are not a full transitive dependency/cycle audit. Add focused import-direction/cycle rules after defining permitted edges; do not claim current enforcement proves every architecture property. |
+| Separate component/global CSS; use CSS Modules | Page/shared components already have colocated CSS. `src/styles.css` deliberately composes them with remaining legacy theme/cascade rules. | **Partly valid.** Reduce cross-screen selector coupling incrementally. Do not mass-convert to CSS Modules or reorder imports: native startup, retained templates and screenshots depend on established selectors/cascade. |
+| Lazy-load expensive modules | Page preparation, guide controller, shared session presentation, detector and optional graphs already use lazy imports. Audio entry still has eager imports from main/controllers. | **Already substantial, not complete.** Measure the real import graph before changing audio loading. Permission timing, microphone readiness, beep timing and interruption behavior must survive any new boundary. |
+| Rename everything into `src/core`, `src/legacy`, adapters and new feature trees | Current published component/page ownership is documented and checked. Pure JS engines are not obsolete simply because they live in `src`. | **Defer mechanical moves.** Choose a module's home when extracting a real responsibility. Avoid temporary dual hierarchies and broad import churn without a functional maintenance benefit. |
+
+## High-risk invariants missing from the proposed map
+
+1. **Atomic persistence:** `deleteGun` deletes all owned records and changes selection in one transaction; `restoreBackup` validates the whole input before one replacement transaction; `exportBackup` reads a consistent multi-store snapshot. Splitting these into independently committed feature operations would change behavior and could leave partial data.
+2. **Startup I/O:** `getLibraryOverview` batches guns, selected ID and device preferences into one read-only snapshot. Replacing it with several feature/provider loads would undo a measured optimization and can produce inconsistent startup state.
+3. **Capture ownership:** keep operation/capture tokens, serialized queues, cancellation and save-failure guards. React mount/unmount or StrictMode must not duplicate microphone acquisition, cues, timers or durable writes.
+4. **Exclusive rendering:** preserve native-to-React handoff, rollback, exact uncontrolled input/file nodes, invalid drafts, focus and delayed/failed chunk behavior. “No DOM manipulation in controllers” is a possible long-term presentation goal, not permission to remove working startup paths now.
+5. **Edition/security boundary:** public creation limits and retained saved-data access must remain; private preview authentication, private assets, no-store/offline policy and Free/Full simulation cannot become a public client-side switch.
+6. **Acceptance:** unchanged visuals, guide frames/GIF content, data schemas/formats, audio/timing behavior, original minified payload/timing budgets and both-edition browser tests. A path refactor should not require visual or performance rebaselining.
+
+## Recommended follow-up order — proposed only
+
+1. **Document seams and strengthen boundary checks.** Inventory the dependencies/closure state to be extracted and permitted import directions. Correct documentation drift: the architecture handoff still says 38 packages, while the checker verifies 39 (13 pages + 26 UI); a Practice controller comment still says six modes although four remain. Keep the completed P0–P8 plan intact and track any approved follow-up separately.
+2. **Extract pure persistence validation.** Move backup/session normalization and validation into internal modules with direct behavior tests and the same public `storage.js` facade. No schema bump, new data copy, transaction restructuring or new provider/store is needed for this slice.
+3. **Extract cohesive orchestration from main, one responsibility at a time.** Start with preferences or gun-library coordination, based on an explicit closure/dependency map. Keep main as composition root; avoid a generic bucket of all view functions or all event handlers. Preserve startup snapshot batching and profile/capture/save locks.
+4. **Consolidate controller presentation where useful.** Extend existing models/ports while keeping engines and service lifecycles independent of React. Native startup and mounted React must consume the same decisions without two simultaneous writers.
+5. **Organize remaining domain files/CSS only where the previous slices reveal a real benefit.** Introduce public module entries and targeted selector scoping without a blanket rename, new routing library, provider hierarchy or duplicate shared folder.
+
+Each implementation slice needs characterization before extraction, unit/coverage and integration checks, public/preview minified builds, pinned Chromium/WebKit screenshot/workflow suites and unchanged original performance gates before b publication. Main/dev remain frozen unless the owner changes that instruction. These steps are recommendations, not started implementation.
+
+## Verification and limits of this review
+
+Read both supplied analyses and inspected the current bootstrap, controllers, pure engines, storage transaction/validation/backup paths, page/shared packages, lazy boundaries, CSS entry, architecture checker, lint configuration and ADR/plan. Current `npm run verify:architecture` passes (39 packages/13 screens); `npm run docs:check` passes (13 screens/83 current captures). No application code, dependency, schema, CSS or runtime behavior was changed, and no refactor was started.
+
+The latest completed full application acceptance remains pre-publication40075428666 and post-push40076234770: 1,017 tests/147 suites and all eight jobs green. Those are prior application evidence, not a newly executed full suite for this read-only review. No fresh benchmark or security audit was performed, so neither the Copilot text nor this review proves a new speedup or a security vulnerability.
+
+Sources: [architecture/entry points](../../architecture/application-structure.md), [ADR001](../../architecture/decisions/001-progressive-react-startup.md), [component map](../../architecture/component-map.json), [ownership contracts](../../architecture/ui-contract-map.md), [completed plan](plan.md), [application sitemap](../../architecture/application-sitemap.md).
+
+## Follow-up: reusable pages versus drill layouts
+
+Owner asked why main/apps/web/src/pages contains only About/Contact and whether DrillSetupPage/DrillRunPage/DrillAboutPage should be shared. Remote refs verified: main6c38bff has only AboutPage/ContactPage in that folder; b2cf044c has all13 page packages. Main remains intentionally unpromoted; no merge is authorized by this question.
+
+Keep complete screen owners under apps/web/src/pages. Reusable layout templates for a specific workflow can live under apps/web/src/features/sessions/layouts, each with source/CSS/tests/export. Proposed names DrillSetupLayout, DrillRunLayout and DrillGuideLayout distinguish shared presentation from screens/routes and independent timing/capture controllers. Existing packages/ui/DrillSetup and live/example PracticeRun/SequenceRun components already provide substantial reuse; extend or compose them rather than adding equivalent second implementations. PracticeGuidePage already serves every supported mode and selected preset.
+
+Suggested relationship: PracticePage and SequencePage compose the shared setup/run templates with their distinct fields, counters, presentation and feature callbacks; PracticeGuidePage composes the guide template and the existing shared run/review example components. AppShell continues to own global header/footer/navigation. Setup/run/review remain states of existing screens; extracting templates does not require new routes or a single merged engine. Consolidate duplicated content where demonstrated; renaming folders alone is not reuse. This is architectural advice only, not authorization to implement or promote main/dev.
+
+## B-only page reuse assessment
+
+Owner narrowed the assessment to b only. Current source already has13 page packages, one AppShell, one common PracticeGuidePage and shared DrillSetup/PracticeRun/SequenceRun presentation. Further recommendations should be based on duplication inside b, not differences from another branch.
+
+The strongest next reusable extraction is the remaining setup content repeated in PracticePage and SequencePage: heading/context composition, fixed/random delay field group, start-sound control and readiness/start area. Extend existing DrillSetup rather than create an equivalent DrillSetupPage alongside it. Preserve per-mode fields, stable input IDs, option defaults and native input ownership. Run components already serve both live screens and guide examples; share only repeated frame/action presentation while retaining different attempt/shot versus completed-sequence counters and independent lifecycle behavior. PracticeGuidePage is already the common guide: a second DrillAboutPage adds no reuse. Keep complete pages in pages and genuinely shared UI in its existing owner; a new layouts directory is useful only for a concrete extracted composition, not a requirement by itself. No implementation changes made.
+
+## B-only assessment: Live Listen, Settings and History
+
+All three already have a dedicated page package (source/CSS/component tests/export), share AppShell and PageHeading and use separate mount/presentation/service ownership. Their content serves different workflows; they should remain separate pages composed from focused components.
+
+- LiveListenPage still contains substantial inline content: mode/sequence panel, listening controls/readiness/sensitivity, microphone level, bounded session timeline and latest recognition/verification. Extract cohesive feature sections when this improves testing/readability; that alone does not make them globally shared. A microphone-level presentation is a plausible shared component with Practice, provided different presentation variants and the existing high-frequency update port remain intact. Transient live timelines and persistent verified History records should not be treated as identical data/components.
+- SettingsPage repeats group headings/descriptions, preference-toggle rows and action rows. A SettingsSection/PreferenceToggle/SettingsAction family would reuse concrete repeated markup. Retain browser-support explanations, gun/device preference scope, the actual native file input and independent private preview subtree; destructive actions keep confirmation/service ownership outside presentation.
+- HistoryPage already composes SavedSessionList, RecognitionList and EvaluationPanel, with separate retained numeric-limit and optional progress content. Further extraction is lower priority; a real duplicated section/status/context primitive may help, but another History wrapper adds little. Preserve draft verification selections, gun ownership, separate history/session deletion and private progress guards.
+
+Across these pages and Practice/Sequence, the gun-context paragraph is a concrete repeated candidate for a shared GunContext component. Extend the existing shared UI home and page-heading/shell contracts; do not add separate per-page headers/footers/navigation or force unlike content into one generic page. Suggested priority for these three pages: Settings repeated rows, then Live Listen feature sections/shared meter, then small History cleanup only if an actual duplicate remains. Review only; no implementation has been authorized by the questions.
